HUMAN: (Owner-authorized; posted by my agent on my behalf.)

Draft PR #141338 is now public for collaboration. The current patch covers multi-term per-device selectors, both result orderings, and consistent stable/incubating/experimental behavior.

@Harsh-128, please feel free to run your independent findings against the concrete diff, especially additional malformed-state edge cases, ordering/interactions, error classification, possible performance effects, and arguments for or against handling zero terms in the same patch. Collaboration is very welcome; the zero-term scope still needs SIG Node guidance.

While reviewing the `AllocationMode: All` path I ran into another allocator path with the same Error-versus-Unschedulable gap. Recording it here because #141338 already touches the same three variants and the shared test harness. I'm not claiming it falls under the accepted malformed-selector scope.

```go
for _, pool := range pools {
	if pool.IsIncomplete {
		return requestData, fmt.Errorf("claim %s, request %s: asks for all devices, but resource pool %s is currently being updated", klog.KObj(claim), request.name(), pool.PoolID)
	}
	if pool.IsInvalid {
		return requestData, fmt.Errorf("claim %s, request %s: asks for all devices, but resource pool %s is currently invalid", klog.KObj(claim), request.name(), pool.PoolID)
	}
```

Neither wraps `ErrFailedAllocationOnNode`, so `DynamicResources.Filter` falls through to its `case err != nil` arm and returns a framework `Error`. That cancels the shared context in `findNodesThatPassFilters`, so a node whose pools are fine loses its result too.

What makes this one different from `createNodeSelector` is that it needs no malformed slice. `IsIncomplete` is set whenever a generation has fewer slices than its declared `resourceSliceCount`, which is the ordinary state during a driver rollout.

The conservative check itself looks intentional to me: a slice that hasn't arrived yet may hold another device matching an `All` request, so the allocator can't know the full set. I would leave that alone and change only the classification. The same three files already wrap the non-`All` invalid-pool fallback with the sentinel, with a comment that allocation on other nodes may proceed, so this brings `All` in line rather than introducing a new policy. `EventsToRegister` also lists `ResourceSlice` Add and Update, so a pod parked as Unschedulable gets woken by the slice update that finishes the rollout.

On tests, the existing `all-devices-of-the-incomplete-pool` case asserts the message with `ContainSubstring` and the sentinel carries an empty string, so following the `"...%w"` pattern keeps the visible text identical. The `expectErrorIs` field you added in #141338 would drop straight onto that case, plus a new invalid-pool one.

@trungminhdo4-glitch, this might be a small extension to #141338 if you and the maintainers would rather keep the per-node classification fixes together. The trigger is different enough that tracking it separately is just as reasonable. Either way I'm not planning a competing PR.

