### Root Cause Analysis

This was introduced in PR #123226 (merged for v1.30, milestone `v1.30`), commit `8854d360a5d`, which added the `STATUS` column to `kubectl get job`.

The status logic in `pkg/printers/internalversion/printers.go` (lines 1277-1292) uses a fallthrough if/else chain that checks for specific Job conditions (`Complete`, `Failed`, `Terminating`, `Suspended`, `FailureTarget`, `SuccessCriteriaMet`). If none of these conditions are true, it defaults to `"Running"`:

```go
var status string
if hasJobCondition(obj.Status.Conditions, batch.JobComplete) {
    status = "Complete"
} else if hasJobCondition(obj.Status.Conditions, batch.JobFailed) {
    status = "Failed"
} else if obj.ObjectMeta.DeletionTimestamp != nil {
    status = "Terminating"
} else if hasJobCondition(obj.Status.Conditions, batch.JobSuspended) {
    status = "Suspended"
} else if hasJobCondition(obj.Status.Conditions, batch.JobFailureTarget) {
    status = "FailureTarget"
} else if hasJobCondition(obj.Status.Conditions, batch.JobSuccessCriteriaMet) {
    status = "SuccessCriteriaMet"
} else {
    status = "Running"
}
```

Interestingly, during the PR review ([comment](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/123226#discussion_r1485622096)) there was discussion about having a `Starting` status (when `status.startTime == nil`), but it was explicitly dropped. The reviewer's reasoning was:

> "Yeah, I agree, let's drop starting for now. IMO it might be confusing, because a natural interpretation from the user's point-of-view is that starting is when there are still pending pods."

So the case where all pods are `Pending` was considered, but the conclusion was to lump it into `Running` to avoid confusion — which arguably created a different kind of confusion (Job says "Running" when nothing is running).

A possible fix would be to check whether `status.ready > 0` or `status.active > 0` with at least one pod actually running, or check `status.startTime`, to distinguish between a truly running Job and one that's still waiting for pods to be scheduled.

