https://github.com/mortent/kubernetes/blob/2e534d6da100ef20a048ed44f9ec9997f64676a7/staging/src/k8s.io/dynamic-resource-allocation/structured/internal/stable/pools_stable.go#L60-L95

That's the new code.  It's then used here:
https://github.com/mortent/kubernetes/blob/2e534d6da100ef20a048ed44f9ec9997f64676a7/staging/src/k8s.io/dynamic-resource-allocation/structured/internal/stable/pools_stable.go#L143

What if we removed the upfront computation and instead double-checked whether a pool is really incomplete?

```Go
	// Find incomplete pools and flatten into a single slice.
	result := make([]*Pool, 0, len(pools))
	for _, pool := range pools {
		isComplete := int64(len(pool.Slices)) == pool.Slices[0].Spec.Pool.ResourceSliceCount
		// If we have all slices, we are done. If not, then we need to start looking for slices
		// which were filtered out above because their node selection made them look irrelevant
		// for the current node. This is necessary for "allocate all" mode (it rejects incomplete
		// pools) and for partitionable devices (the extra slices may contain counter set definitions
		// or information about other devices which reduce counters).
		if !isComplete {
			...
			... maybe set isComplete = true ...
		}
		pool.IsIncomplete = !isComplete
```

For the vast majority of current real-world ResourceSlices, we will directly set `isComplete := int64(len(pool.Slices)) == pool.Slices[0].Spec.Pool.ResourceSliceCount = true` because we have all slices and all of them are for the node.

> This actually becomes an even larger issue for Partitionable Devices. Since we are scoping the counter sets to a pool, the allocator needs to know about all allocated devices that consumes counters in that pool, meaning we need to know about allocated devices on other nodes than the current one.
>
> I'm wondering if an option here is to say that all ResourceSlices in a pool must have the same node selector?

We cannot make that a requirement for GA ResourceSlices because it wasn't required before. We would have to do a phased transition where we announce this, but then tolerate other slices for several releases.

I think we can instead make it a recommendation and (hopefully) with the approach above will handle ResourceSlices which follow the recommendation efficiently. The `if !isComplete` then is only necessary for the exceptions and thus rare. Or am I missing use cases where it is necessary to do the fallback regularly?

Ok, checking ... when a pod is configured with hostNetwork: true, it does not get its own network namespace. Instead, it shares the network namespace of the host node. So when the kubelet prepares to execute the httpGet preStop hook it tries to determine the target IP., that is empty for hostNetwork pods and fails.

HostNetwork Pods are using the kubelet hostIP as PodIPs

https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/8ac5701d3a146b367c83449d1eb6d872c003752e/pkg/kubelet/kubelet_pods.go#L1998-L2014

So we could try to implement the same logic and use the HostIP to default the probe host, however, this present some inconvenients from my perspective:

- The node IP might be publicly exposed, that seems is an unnecessary risk, and using it by default seem to contradict https://github.com/kubernetes/enhancements/issues/4940
- A host can have multiple, dynamic, or virtual IP addresses, we have recurrent problems with this logic that now depends on a external controller when using cloud providers, that will impact hostNetwork pods startup on those scenarios, that will fail until the cloud provider configure the addresses

IMHO the most robust and secure solution is to use the fact that both the kubelet process and the hostNetwork pod are running in the same network namespace, guaranteeing that kubelet can reliably reach the pod's container using the loopback interface `localhost` (using 127.0.0.1 only works for IPv4 ;)

I suggest implementing something as:

```go
// This function determines the host and podIP for a probe or hook.
func (kl *Kubelet) getHookIP(pod *v1.Pod) (string, error) {
	// For hostNetwork pods, the runtime does not provide a sandbox IP.
	// Since the kubelet executes the hook from the host's network namespace,
	// which is the same namespace as the pod's, we can safely and securely
	// use the loopback address. This avoids the complexity and security
	// concerns of using the node's primary IP(s).
	if pod.Spec.HostNetwork {
		return "localhost", nil
	}

	// For non-hostNetwork pods, fall back to the existing logic of querying
	// the container runtime for the pod's assigned IP address.
	status, err := kl.containerManager.GetPodStatus(pod.UID, pod.Name, pod.Namespace)
	if err != nil {
		klog.ErrorS(err, "Unable to get pod info, event handlers may be invalid", "pod", klog.KObj(pod))
		return "", err
	}

	// Ensure that at least one IP is present in the status.
	if len(status.IPs) == 0 {
		return "", fmt.Errorf("failed to find network status or pod IP for pod: %v", klog.KObj(pod))
	}

	// Use the primary IP from the pod status.
	return status.IPs[0], nil
}
```

